_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 13
---
order: 13
---
short_label: III.13
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: A circle does not touch another circle at more than one point whether it touches it internally or externally.
----
proof:

For, if possible, let the circle {ABDC} touch the circle *EBFD,* first internally, at more points than one, namely {D} and {B}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="260" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.13a diagram" src="propIII13a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Two impossible figures, one per canvas, each with its own walk: this
    // one is the internal case, canvas_1 the external. The proof prose
    // between the canvases binds to canvas_0 by DOM order and everything
    // after canvas_1 (the external case and the guide) to canvas_1, which
    // is what each passage means, so no ref needs a canvas selector.
    //
    // The inner "circle" EBFD is Joyce's four-arc figure (arcTR, arcRS,
    // arcSU, arcUT) — it cannot be a circle, which is the point. There is
    // no single element for a {NAME} ref to bind, so EBFD stays literal in
    // the prose; the slides light all four arcs together.
    //
    // ABD is the random-filled outer circle. The prose calls it ABDC (and
    // once ABCD), so a fully transparent twin carries those names; the
    // filled one is never in a slide set, since a highlighted face is
    // promoted over the arcs' region (#140). BG, GD, HD, BH are the parts
    // of BD the argument compares — transparent twins over the drawn line.
    // Resting figure pixel-unchanged.
    var ARCS  = ["arcTR","arcRS","arcSU","arcUT"];
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","D","E","F","ABD","ABDC"].concat(ARCS);
    var CENT  = GIVEN.concat(["G","H"]);
    var LINE  = CENT.concat(["BD"]);
    var ARG   = LINE.concat(["BG","GD","HD"]);
    var ARG2  = ARG.concat(["BH"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.13a",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;150,40",
            "B;point;free;40,170",
            "D;point;free;220,170",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            "M;point;midpoint;BD;0;0",
            "ABD;circle;circumcircle;A,B,D;0;0;black;random",
            "O;point;center;ABD;0;0",
            "C;point;circleSlider;ABD,200,70;black;0",
            "N;point;extend;M,O,M,O;0;0",
            "P;point;midpoint;O,B;0;0",
            "Q;point;midpoint;O,D;0;0",
            "S;point;extend;N,P,P,B;0;0",
            "U;point;extend;N,Q,Q,D;0;0",
            "W;point;intersection;P,Q,N,O;0;0",
            "V;point;extend;N,W,N,W;0;0",
            "G;point;intersection;V,P,B,D",
            "H;point;intersection;V,Q,B,D",
            "R;point;extend;V,P,P,B;0;0",
            "T;point;extend;V,Q,Q,D;0;0",
            "E;point;cutoff;V,N,V,R;black;0",
            "F;point;cutoff;N,V,N,S;black;0",
            "arcTR;sector;sector;V,T,R;0;0;black;0",
            "arcRS;sector;sector;P,R,S;0;0;black;0",
            "arcSU;sector;sector;N,S,U;0;0;black;0",
            "arcUT;sector;sector;Q,U,T;0;0;black;0",
            // Transparent twin of the outer circle, under the prose name.
            "ABDC;circle;circumcircle;A,B,D;0;0;0;0",
            // Segments of BD the argument compares; BD already draws them.
            "BG;line;connect;B,G;0;0;0",
            "GD;line;connect;G,D;0;0;0",
            "HD;line;connect;H,D;0;0;0",
            "BH;line;connect;B,H;0;0;0"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, D, C — all points
        // of the given circles, introduced on slide 1, so nothing is
        // deferred. G and H are intersections (derived) and are listed per
        // slide; E and F are cutoffs likewise, but they only label the
        // inner figure and belong to slide 1.
        aliases: { "ABCD": "ABDC", "BGHD": "BD", "GB": "BG", "DG": "GD",
                   "DH": "HD", "HB": "BH" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”).
            { text: "A circle does not touch another circle at more than one point whether it touches it internally or externally.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.13" }] },
            { text: "For, if possible, let the circle {ABDC} touch the circle EBFD, first internally, at more points than one, namely {D} and {B}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["ABDC","D","B"].concat(ARCS) },
            { text: "Take the center {G} of the circle {ABDC} and the center {H} of EBFD.",
              visible: CENT,
              highlighted: ["G", "H"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "G", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "H", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the centers of the circles", ref: "III.1" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the straight line joined from {G} to {H} falls on {B} and {D}.",
              visible: CENT,
              highlighted: ["G","H","B","D"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the line of centers falls on the point of contact", ref: "III.11" } ] },
            { text: "Let it so fall, as {BGHD}.",
              visible: LINE,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "BD", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] } },
            { text: "Then, since the point {G} is the center of the circle {ABCD} and {BG} equals {GD}, therefore {BG} is greater than {HD}.",
              visible: ARG,
              highlighted: ["G","BG","GD","HD"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "BG and GD are radii of the circle ABDC", ref: "I.Def.15" },
                  { claim: "HD is a part of GD",                     ref: "C.N.5" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore {BH} is much greater than {HD}.",
              visible: ARG2,
              highlighted: ["BH","HD"] },
            { text: "Again, since the point {H} is the center of the circle EBFD, {BH} equals {HD}, but it was also proved much greater than it, which is impossible.",
              visible: ARG2,
              highlighted: ["H","BH","HD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BH and HD are radii of the circle EBFD", ref: "I.Def.15" }] },
            { text: "Therefore a circle does not touch a circle internally at more points than one.",
              visible: ARG2,
              highlighted: ["ABDC"].concat(ARCS) }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.13") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup, shared by both canvases' decks.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1":  "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Def.15":  "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15/",
        "C.N.5":     "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn5/",
        "III.1":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/",
        "III.2":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII2/",
        "III.11":    "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII11/",
        "III.Def.3": "/elements/books/bookIII/definitions/defIII3/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Take the center {G} of the circle {ABDC} and the center {H} of *EBFD.* [!just III.1]

Therefore the straight line joined from {G} to {H} falls on {B} and {D}. [!just III.11]

Let it so fall, as {BGHD}.

Then, since the point {G} is the center of the circle {ABCD} and {BG} equals {GD}, therefore {BG} is greater than {HD}.  Therefore {BH} is much greater than {HD}.

Again, since the point {H} is the center of the circle *EBFD,* {BH} equals {HD}, but it was also proved much greater than it, which is impossible.

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="270" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.13b diagram" src="propIII13b.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The external case. ACK is a 4-field circle — no colour fields, so it
    // carries the opaque near-white default face, and it sits over the
    // lens it shares with the blue circle. It is left exactly as
    // converted and never named in a highlighted set: a highlighted face
    // is promoted over everything behind it (#140). ACKh is its fully
    // transparent twin, and the prose reaches it with {ACK|ACKh}. ABDC is
    // the transparent twin of the filled ABC under the prose's name.
    // Resting figure pixel-unchanged.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","D","ABC","ABDC"];
    var TOUCH = GIVEN.concat(["K","ACK","ACKh"]);
    var JOIN  = TOUCH.concat(["AC"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.13b",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;140,100",
            "B;point;free;50,220",
            "C;point;free;170,140",
            "ABC;circle;circumcircle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "D;point;circleSlider;ABC,200,230;black;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "M;point;midpoint;A,C;0;0",
            "KK';line;perpendicular;M,A;0;0;0",
            "K;point;lineSlider;KK',220,40;black",
            "ACK;circle;circumcircle;A,C,K",
            // Transparent twins: the blue circle under the prose's name,
            // and the highlight target standing in for ACK.
            "ABDC;circle;circumcircle;A,B,C;0;0;0;0",
            "ACKh;circle;circumcircle;A,C,K;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C, D, K. The first
        // four are the given circle's points; K is the lineSlider that
        // fixes the second circle, which arrives on slide 2, so it is
        // deferred out of the auto-union.
        deferDraggables: ["K"],
        aliases: { "ABCD": "ABDC", "CA": "AC" },
        slides: [
            { text: "I say further that neither does it so touch it externally.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["ABDC"] },
            { text: "For, if possible, let the circle {ACK|ACKh} touch the circle {ABDC} at more points than one, namely {A} and {C}.",
              visible: TOUCH,
              highlighted: ["ACKh","ABDC","A","C"] },
            { text: "Join {AC}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "AC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Then, since on the circumference of each of the circles {ABDC} and {ACK|ACKh} two points {A} and {C} have been taken at random, the straight line joining the points falls within each circle,",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["A","C","AC"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "a straight line joining two points on a circle falls within it", ref: "III.2" } ] },
            { text: "but it fell within the circle {ABCD} and outside {ACK|ACKh}, which is absurd.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["AC","ABDC","ACKh"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "circles that touch do not cut one another", ref: "III.Def.3" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore a circle does not touch a circle externally at more points than one. And it was proved that neither does it so touch it internally.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: ["ABDC","ACKh"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
</script>
</figure>

Therefore a circle does not touch a circle internally at more points than one.

<hr width="30%">

I say further that neither does it so touch it externally.

For, if possible, let the circle {ACK|ACKh} touch the circle {ABDC} at more points than one, namely {A} and {C}.  Join {AC}.

Then, since on the circumference of each of the circles {ABDC} and {ACK|ACKh} two points {A} and {C} have been taken at random, the straight line joining the points falls within each circle, but it fell within the circle {ABCD} and outside {ACK|ACKh}, which is absurd. [!just III.2; III.Def.3]

Therefore a circle does not touch a circle externally at more points than one.

And it was proved that neither does it so touch it internally.

Therefore *a circle does not touch another circle at more than one point whether it touches it internally or externally.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

In the second impossible figure there are three curves connecting {A} to {C}.  The two circles are not supposed to cut each other, but just to touch each other at the two points {A} and {C}, and the straight line {AC} should lie between the two circles and not within either one.

There are logical flaws in this proof similar to those in the last two proofs.

This proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
